   Vol tops the ranked list (VIXCLS -1.14 pts). The equity-internals/sector layer shows the anomalous divergence: Semi-Cap/EDA -8.2 and Cybersecurity -9.9 vs a barely-up SPY. This is a premarket/close phase run with frozen data. I have my numbers.
   
   {"headline":"Vol bleeds back to 15.2 as the tape holds still, but a savage semi-cap and cybersecurity air-pocket screams underneath a calm index","tape":[{"label":"VIX","value":"15.2","change":"-1.14 pts","positive":true},{"label":"10Y","value":"4.79","change":"+0 bp","positive":true},{"label":"HY OAS","value":"2.66","change":"+1 bp","positive":false}],"schedule":[{"et":"2026-09-10","event":"ADBE earnings (post)","consensus":"implied move 6.9333%","group":"Next week"},{"et":"2026-09-10","event":"ORCL earnings (post)","consensus":"implied move 9.8619%","group":"Next week"},{"et":"2026-09-16","event":"FOMC 9/16","consensus":"HIKE 60%","prior":"3.75-4.00%","group":"FOMC"},{"et":"2026-09-30","event":"MU earnings (post)","consensus":"implied move 12.373%","group":"Later"},{"et":"2026-10-28","event":"FOMC 10/28","consensus":"HOLD 70%","prior":"3.50-3.75%","group":"FOMC"},{"et":"2026-12-09","event":"FOMC 12/9","consensus":"HIKE 64%","prior":"3.75-4.00%","group":"FOMC"}],"sections":[{"title":"Falling vol, not a moving index, is what defines today's regime","body":"The one input that moved is volatility itself. VIXCLS printed 15.2 against a prior 16.34, a -1.14 pt drop (obs 2026-09-02), the widest single move on the ranked board and roughly 1.7x its own 20-session median. Nothing else on the front rank did work: DGS10 sat at 4.79 versus 4.79, a +0 bp non-move (obs 2026-09-02), and HY OAS at 2.66 versus 2.65 is a +1 bp shuffle (BAMLH0A0HYM2, obs 2026-09-02; fredDirect fetch failed, so this is argon's daily mirror running 5 days behind). This is a calm-tape regime: risk is being priced cheaper without the index having to rally to earn it. The frozen-data caveat below governs any tape-level number. Who benefits from falling vol, ranked by duration: long-dated equity risk and premium sellers first, as the discount rate on convexity compresses; short-dated hedges bleed hardest. The policy backdrop stays hawkish-tilted — Frenzy Capital fed-funds futures via argon (not CME FedWatch, snapshot 2026-09-02) put the 9/16 FOMC at a 60% hike, implied rate 3.78, range 3.75-4.00%. A falling VIX into a live hike-probability meeting is the tension worth watching."},{"title":"The real anomaly: a violent semi-cap and cybersecurity collapse hiding under a flat index","body":"Beside the quiet vol print sits the single most jarring divergence on the frame — sector dispersion that a benign VIX completely masks. Semi-Cap/EDA fell -8.2% on the week, -8.4% versus SPY with all 8 of 8 members down (AMAT, ARM, ASML, CDNS, KLAC, LRCX, SNPS, TER; asOf 2026-09-03), and Cybersecurity was worse at -9.9%, -10.1% versus SPY, 4 of 4 down (NET, S, CRWD, PANW). Semi-Logic/ASIC added -4.6% (-4.8% vs SPY, 6 of 6). Against that, SPY's own weekly excess was a rounding error and Devices/Endpoint (TSLA, AAPL) actually ran +5.2%, +4.9% vs SPY. Rotation confirms the split: XLK is the weakest sector at -1.4% (1w) while the el-nino-ag-2026 theme leads at +7.6%, +7.3% excess (asOf 2026-09-03). So a headline index doing nothing and a VIX at 15.2 conceal a genuine risk unwind inside semiconductor capital equipment and security software — the parts of tech with the longest cash-flow duration, which is exactly what a still-hawkish 60% hike probability into 9/16 punishes first. Coverage note on the tape: Unusual Whales' market tide, flow and tape are frozen outside RTH; the flow number on the frame carries session stamp 2026-09-03T16:10:00-04:00 and is that prior session's, not a live read."}]}
